{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2100 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d337b11c329b579619e220f5c2d7f7025e6512392eafcedcd2821328c171a2f5" :edb/db-before-t 2099 :edb/db-after-t 2100 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535412 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:35:53.230Z" 13194139535412 true] [17592186147288 1100 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?\n\n1. $y'' + 81y = 0$\n2. $y'' + 9y'+81y = 0$\n3. $y'' - 81y = 0$" 13194139535412 false] [17592186147288 1100 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?\n\n- $y'' + 81y = 0$\n\n- $y'' + 9y'+81y = 0$\n\n- $y'' - 81y = 0$" 13194139535412 true] [17592186147288 1145 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I takes the form\n$y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nwith\n$\\omega^2 = 81 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 9$.\nTherefore, equation I represents SHM.\n- Equation II does not take the form\n$y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nbecause it has a $y'$ term.\n- Equation III does not take the form\n$y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0$.\nIt would require $\\omega^2 = -81$, which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535412 false] [17592186147288 1145 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I takes the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 81 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I represents SHM.\n\n- Equation II does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nbecause it has a $y'$ term.\n\n- Equation III does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt would require $\\omega^2 = -81,$ which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535412 true] [17592186147289 1100 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 100y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" 13194139535412 true] [17592186147289 1100 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 100y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" 13194139535412 false] [17592186147289 1145 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere the period $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 100\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 10\n$$\n\nso the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{10} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\,\\textrm{s}\n$$" 13194139535412 false] [17592186147289 1145 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere the period $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 100\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 10,\n$$\n\nso the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{10} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\,\\textrm{s}.\n$$" 13194139535412 true] [17592186147290 1100 "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 9 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y = R\\cos(3t+\\phi_1)$\n\n- $y = R\\sin(9t+\\phi_2)$\n\n- $y = A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3t}$\n\nNote that $R,\\phi_1,\\phi_2, A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139535412 true] [17592186147290 1100 "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 9 y = 0$.\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y = R\\cos(3t+\\phi_1)$\n2. $y = R\\sin(9t+\\phi_2)$\n3. $y = A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3t}$\n\nNote that $R,\\phi_1,\\phi_2, A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139535412 false] [17592186147290 1145 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = 0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1. $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n2. $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n3. $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 9\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\omega=3$ into the three different forms of the general solution gives the following:\n\n1. $y = R\\cos{(3t + \\phi_1)}$\n2. $y = R\\sin{(3t + \\phi_2)}$\n3. $y =A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3 t}$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t)$." 13194139535412 false] [17592186147290 1145 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = 0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 9\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\omega=3$ into the three different forms of the general solution gives the following:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(3t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(3t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y =A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3 t}$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$" 13194139535412 true] [17592186147291 1100 "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 36y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$, find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139535412 false] [17592186147291 1100 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 36y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\textrm{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139535412 true] [17592186147291 1145 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nand the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)}\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 36\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 6\n$$\n\nso the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(6t+\\phi)}\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0) = 0$. Substituting this into the equation above gives\n\n$$\nR\\sin\\phi = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\phi = 0\n$$\n\nSince $\\phi=0$, our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{6 t}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ when $t=0$, which means that $y'(0) = 3$. Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = 6R\\cos{6t}\n$$\n\nand substituting $y'(0) = 3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3 = 6R\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad R=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$" 13194139535412 false] [17592186147291 1145 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0,\n$$\n\nand the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)},\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 36\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 6,\n$$\n\nso the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(6t+\\phi)}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0) = 0.$ Substituting this into the equation above gives\n\n$$\nR\\sin\\phi = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\phi = 0.\n$$\n\nSince $\\phi=0,$ our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{6 t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that $y'(0) = 3.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = 6R\\cos{6t},\n$$\n\nand substituting $y'(0) = 3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3 = 6R\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad R=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\textrm{m}.$" 13194139535412 true] [17592186147292 1100 "A particle of mass $36\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.\n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The governing differential equation is $y'' + \\dfrac{1}{2}y = 0$\n2. The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi}\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}$\n3. The general solution can be expressed as $y = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)}$, where $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase." 13194139535412 false] [17592186147292 1100 "A particle of mass $36\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is $y'' + \\dfrac{1}{2}y = 0$\n\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi}\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}$\n\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)},$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase." 13194139535412 true] [17592186147292 1145 "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m=36$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k=9$ has\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = \\dfrac{k}{m} = \\dfrac{9}{36} = \\dfrac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nso its equation of motion is \n\n$$\ny'' + \\dfrac{1}{4}y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is different from the equation in statement I.\n\n- Statement II is true. The frequency $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi} \\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\\,R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1}),\\,R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $\\omega = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\phi_1 = \\phi$ in the form $R\\sin{(\\omega t+\\phi_2)}$ gives\n\n$$\ny(t) = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139535412 true] [17592186147292 1145 "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m=36$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k=9$ has\n$\\omega^2 = \\dfrac{k}{m} = \\dfrac{9}{36} = \\dfrac{1}{4}$,\nso its equation of motion is\n$y'' + \\dfrac{1}{4}y = 0$,\nwhich is different from the equation in statement I.\n- Statement II is true. The frequency $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) is given by\n$f = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi} \\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- Statement III is true. The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms:\n$A\\cos ωt + B\\sin ωt, R\\cos (ωt + ϕ_{1}), R\\sin (ωt + ϕ_{2})$.\nWriting $\\omega = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\phi_1 = \\phi$ in the form $R\\sin{(\\omega t+\\phi_2)}$ gives\n$y(t) = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139535412 false] [17592186147369 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347791 13194139535412 true] [17592186147369 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347854 13194139535412 true] [17592186147369 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347861 13194139535412 true] [17592186147369 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347868 13194139535412 true] [17592186147370 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347798 13194139535412 true] [17592186147370 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347833 13194139535412 true] [17592186147370 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347875 13194139535412 true] [17592186147370 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347882 13194139535412 true] [17592186147371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347805 13194139535412 true] [17592186147371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347840 13194139535412 true] [17592186147371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347889 13194139535412 true] [17592186147371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347896 13194139535412 true] [17592186147371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347903 13194139535412 true] [17592186147372 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347812 13194139535412 true] [17592186147372 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347847 13194139535412 true] [17592186147372 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347910 13194139535412 true] [17592186147372 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347917 13194139535412 true] [17592186147373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347819 13194139535412 true] [17592186147373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347826 13194139535412 true] [17592186147373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347924 13194139535412 true] [17592186147373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347931 13194139535412 true] [17592186148077 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535412 true] [17592186148077 1100 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" 13194139535412 false] [17592186148077 1100 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" 13194139535412 true] [17592186148077 1145 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents SHM is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nIn the above case:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\n$$" 13194139535412 true] [17592186148079 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535412 true] [17592186148079 1100 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" 13194139535412 true] [17592186148079 1100 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 16y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" 13194139535412 false] [17592186148079 1145 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ is given by:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\omega }\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" 13194139535412 true] [17592186148081 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535412 true] [17592186148081 1100 "A particle of mass $12\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=24\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139535412 true] [17592186148081 1100 "A particle of mass $12kg$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 24kg/s^{2}$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139535412 false] [17592186148081 1145 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{24}{12}=2.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0.\n$$" 13194139535412 true] [17592186148088 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535412 true] [17592186148088 1100 "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 144y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8m/s$, find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139535412 false] [17592186148088 1100 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+144y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139535412 true] [17592186148088 1145 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=144\\,⟹\\,\\omega =12.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡12t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=8.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=12R\\operatorname{cos}⁡12t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n8=12R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡12t.\n$$" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347791 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347792 13194139535412 true] [17592186347791 1089 "q-64247" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347791 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535412 true] [17592186347791 1100 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347791 1113 #uuid "ff1986bf-d262-5ccf-8919-3cde9e5e181f" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347791 1145 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents SHM is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nIn the above case:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3\n$$" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347792 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347793 13194139535412 true] [17592186347792 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347794 13194139535412 true] [17592186347792 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347795 13194139535412 true] [17592186347792 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347796 13194139535412 true] [17592186347792 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347797 13194139535412 true] [17592186347792 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535412 true] [17592186347792 1041 "selection" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347792 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347796 13194139535412 true] [17592186347792 1110 #uuid "19c98ad8-be18-5fd1-aab4-eaf5508cc437" 131941395354