$\n{y}^{″}+81y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139535412 true] [17592186347805 1113 #uuid "30ec0217-3ff6-524b-880d-7c9a61fc1c5e" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347805 1145 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=81\\,⟹\\,\\omega =9.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347806 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347807 13194139535412 true] [17592186347806 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347808 13194139535412 true] [17592186347806 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347809 13194139535412 true] [17592186347806 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347810 13194139535412 true] [17592186347806 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347811 13194139535412 true] [17592186347806 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535412 true] [17592186347806 1041 "selection" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347806 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347808 13194139535412 true] [17592186347806 1110 #uuid "4498c0e7-b4fc-5b12-acbd-8efa606ebc8a" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347807 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347807 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535412 true] [17592186347807 1111 #uuid "c018f2d7-d7c6-587e-8860-51a4962fe485" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347808 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347808 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535412 true] [17592186347808 1111 #uuid "82bc6f20-9b80-568f-89c4-2a91a55f615a" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347809 1034 "I only" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347809 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535412 true] [17592186347809 1111 #uuid "300647d7-9ca6-5331-a12f-c7402de2fb3b" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347810 1034 "III only" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347810 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535412 true] [17592186347810 1111 #uuid "90aff64b-40ad-5d28-9a78-e508958c5a1a" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347811 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347811 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535412 true] [17592186347811 1111 #uuid "c15ac956-9a5f-5f52-a0ea-53595b72ad32" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347812 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347813 13194139535412 true] [17592186347812 1089 "q-64253" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347812 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535412 true] [17592186347812 1100 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+64y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139535412 true] [17592186347812 1113 #uuid "d81de893-b23d-5af6-ac6e-27b0b49baeee" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347812 1145 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=64\\,⟹\\,\\omega =8.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8R\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4=8R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t.\n$$" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347814 13194139535412 true] [17592186347813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347815 13194139535412 true] [17592186347813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347816 13194139535412 true] [17592186347813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347817 13194139535412 true] [17592186347813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347818 13194139535412 true] [17592186347813 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535412 true] [17592186347813 1041 "selection" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347813 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347814 13194139535412 true] [17592186347813 1110 #uuid "ea1b4a19-42fd-5d9c-b6cf-9c3aef296fc4" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347814 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347814 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535412 true] [17592186347814 1111 #uuid "4a95dfd5-717e-5439-a97e-19e8ae4e8ae2" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347815 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{m}" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347815 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535412 true] [17592186347815 1111 #uuid "c650af33-cd36-5197-8a7f-797cfc76480d" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347816 1034 "4\\,\\text{m}" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347816 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535412 true] [17592186347816 1111 #uuid "60acd33a-75c3-58a1-b031-34dff62a081c" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347817 1034 "1\\,\\text{m}" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347817 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535412 true] [17592186347817 1111 #uuid "1925c158-9f84-5270-826c-0ad56fbc5851" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347818 1034 "2\\,\\text{m}" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347818 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535412 true] [17592186347818 1111 #uuid "fe9b034a-f4e4-50ff-a31a-b8115cfd73b8" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347819 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347820 13194139535412 true] [17592186347819 1089 "q-64255" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347819 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535412 true] [17592186347819 1100 "A particle of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=32\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347819 1113 #uuid "b407710d-82b6-5413-b053-42b11d925365" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347819 1145 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{32}{8}=4.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347821 13194139535412 true] [17592186347820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347822 13194139535412 true] [17592186347820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347823 13194139535412 true] [17592186347820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347824 13194139535412 true] [17592186347820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347825 13194139535412 true] [17592186347820 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535412 true] [17592186347820 1041 "selection" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347820 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347825 13194139535412 true] [17592186347820 1110 #uuid "544c537f-d29d-559f-97f9-d59051c19179" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347821 1034 "{y}^{″}+2y=0" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347821 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535412 true] [17592186347821 1111 #uuid "532f318f-ffb1-56ce-a0c7-d7d7e51f14eb" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347822 1034 "{y}^{″}-4y=0" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347822 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535412 true] [17592186347822 1111 #uuid "1e937a2f-6b2f-5d0e-ba64-cd4bab91487e" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347823 1034 "{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{2}y=0" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347823 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535412 true] [17592186347823 1111 #uuid "79e7f7fd-2ece-5807-8865-a9add8e72f3a" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347824 1034 "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}y=0" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347824 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535412 true] [17592186347824 1111 #uuid "0aa56111-5e00-5839-99bf-f8f70c82ed0b" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347825 1034 "{y}^{″}+4y=0" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347825 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535412 true] [17592186347825 1111 #uuid "f5fc45a7-8311-5d8e-aa58-c9605249b0b4" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347826 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347827 13194139535412 true] [17592186347826 1089 "q-64258" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347826 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535412 true] [17592186347826 1100 "A particle of mass $32\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{4}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{4}$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." 13194139535412 true] [17592186347826 1113 #uuid "159cb1ab-7613-5b55-bdef-c9e84b2bbb54" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347826 1145 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{8}{32}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535412 true] [17592186347827 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347828 13194139535412 true] [17592186347827 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347829 13194139535412 true] [17592186347827 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347830 13194139535412 true] [17592186347827 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347831 13194139535412 true] [17592186347827 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347832 13194139535412 true] [17592186347827 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535412 true] [17592186347827 1041 "selection" 13194139535412 true] [17592186347827 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347831 13194139535412 true] [1759218634782